  m_Name: AircraftLandingGearData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Aircraft Landing Gear
  engineDescription: An Aircraft Landing Gear System is a mechanical system that
    supports an aircraft during landing, takeoff, taxiing, and ground operations
    while absorbing the impact forces generated during touchdown. Its main components
    include landing gear struts, wheels, tires, shock absorbers, hydraulic actuators,
    brakes, bearings, retraction mechanisms, and control linkages. During operation,
    the landing gear is extended before landing to provide a stable support structure
    for the aircraft. During touchdown, the shock-absorbing mechanism absorbs and
    dissipates landing impact energy, while the wheels rotate to allow the aircraft
    to decelerate smoothly. After takeoff, the landing gear can be retracted into
    the aircraft structure to reduce aerodynamic drag. Aircraft landing gear systems
    are widely used in commercial aircraft, military aircraft, helicopters, and general
    aviation aircraft. From an engineering perspective, understanding impact loading,
    structural strength, shock absorption, hydraulic actuation, braking, fatigue,
    vibration, aerodynamic drag, and maintenance inspection is essential for ensuring
    safe and reliable aircraft operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
